<article class="guide about">
    <header class="listing-header">
        <p class="eyebrow">About</p>
        <h1>Why Relay exists</h1>
        <p class="lede">Most useful work depends on one person more than anyone admits. Relay exists to make the next person findable before that becomes a problem.</p>
    </header>

    <div class="guide-body">
        <p>A tool for accountants, a home heating control system, a small internal service: each works because someone understands it, answers the awkward questions and fixes it when it breaks. The same fact that makes it useful also makes it hard to sell, fund, staff or hand over.</p>

        <p>The quickest route to the people who need it usually runs through someone else — a professional register, an installer network, a distributor, an acquirer. Each is being asked to take on risk. If the person behind the product is unavailable, who supports it? What happens to its users? Without a credible answer, the gatekeeper waits, the business case stalls, and the work stays small.</p>

        <p>That gap is not only an end-of-life problem. Continuity is the missing piece that makes the next step possible: the first serious customer, the first investor, the first hire, the first partner. A plan that names who else can carry the work turns a single point of failure into something another person can assess.</p>

        <p>Relay is a registry for that moment. An owner describes what they have — or what they want to exist — and what someone taking it on would inherit: the support burden, the access, the economics and the obligations. A collaborator, successor, tester, buyer or temporary steward can then decide whether to take it on. The introduction happens here; the handover remains the project's own.</p>

        <p>It is deliberately not a marketplace. Relay does not broker deals, hold money or certify that a transfer happened. It is free and open source, and small on purpose: listings expire after six months unless the owner confirms they are still needed, and a person can reject, veto or take down anything that does not belong.</p>

        <p>If you are building something that needs someone next — or you are the someone — start with a listing. The brief is the part that makes the risk legible.</p>

        <p class="actions">
            <a class="button" href="/find/">Browse listings</a>
            <a class="button button-secondary" href="/submit/">List a project or idea</a>
        </p>
    </div>
</article>
